# Mandatory Cursor Tooling Stack

Always follow this stack for current and future projects in this workspace.

## Active tools
1. **token-optimizer** — personal skill at `~/.cursor/skills/token-optimizer` (also token-coach, token-dashboard, fleet-auditor, resume-checkpoint). Prefer compact context, avoid redundant reads, use measure/doctor when optimizing tokens.
2. **awesome-cursorrules** — library at `~/.cursor/tooling/awesome-cursorrules`. Project rules live in `.cursor/rules/`. Prefer matching stack rules over inventing new style guides.
3. **paritok** — compression gateway at `~/.cursor/tooling/paritok`. When the user routes Cursor/OpenAI through Paritok (`OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080`), keep prompts lean and do not expand compressed `[REF:…]` blobs unless needed.

## Hard requirements
- Keep changes scoped; do not over-engineer.
- Prefer existing project patterns over rewriting architecture.
- Do not dump entire rule libraries into context — use the curated `.cursor/rules` files.
- When creating a **new project**, copy rules from `~/.cursor/tooling/project-rules-template/rules` into that project's `.cursor/rules/` and keep this alwaysApply rule.
- Do not commit secrets (`.env`, tokens, credentials).
- On Windows, native Token Optimizer Cursor hooks may be unavailable; still follow the skill guidance manually.

## Python projects
Follow the Python rules already present in `.cursor/rules/` (typing, pytest, Ruff/PEP8, modular layout).
